fact WorkItem(id: "WI-785", description: some(value: "A destructuring LAMBDA cannot match a NAME-keyed tuple -- `match_tuple_pattern` read only `Value::Tuple.pos`, so a named tuple presented as ZERO components, failed the arity test, and raised at eval on a program that loaded clean. MEASURED (fresh Interpreter per case): with `operation apply_tuple(f: Function[A = (acc: Int64, x: Int64), B = Int64]) -> Int64 = f((acc: 3, x: 10))`, passing `lambda (acc, x) -> acc - x` RAISED, while all three neighbours worked -- the positional tuple type `Function[A = (Int64, Int64), B]` with the same lambda gave Ok(-7), an OPERATION taking one `(acc: Int64, x: Int64)` parameter gave Ok(-7), and a non-destructuring `lambda t -> t.acc - t.x` gave Ok(-7). So exactly one corner was broken: destructuring-binder over name-keyed tuple. ROOT CAUSE: eval/pattern.rs `match_tuple_pattern` matched `Value::Tuple { pos, .. }` and compared `pos.len()` against the binder count, discarding the value's `named` components entirely; a name-keyed tuple carries all of its components in `named` and none in `pos`. The sibling `match_constructor_pattern` in the same file already had the right shape -- `constructor_sub_values` presents positional-then-named so the sub-pattern loop is keying-agnostic, plus WI-445 label resolution for named sub-patterns -- so this was an asymmetry between two adjacent matchers, not a missing concept. SECOND SILENT GAP FIXED WITH IT: the dispatch arm read `Pattern::Tuple { positional, .. }` and dropped the PATTERN's own `named` sub-patterns on the floor, so a labelled tuple pattern bound nothing and still reported a match -- a silent skip against the repo's loud-error principle. FIX: present the scrutinee's components as positional-then-named and match arity-strictly, with labelled sub-patterns resolved by LABEL, mirroring `match_constructor_pattern`. Indexing components positionally is well-defined precisely BECAUSE a named tuple is an ORDERED PRODUCT -- `canonicalize_record_named_args` (kb/resolve.rs:3842) returns early for one, documented \"source order IS its identity\", so component i is the i-th component as spelled. VERIFIED it is source order and not alphabetical: a tuple written `(x: 3, acc: 10)` (whose alphabetical order is acc,x) destructured by `lambda (p, q) -> p - q` yields -7, i.e. p bound the FIRST WRITTEN component; alphabetical binding would have yielded +7. Arity strictness verified too: a 3-component tuple against a 2-binder pattern still does not match. This does NOT re-conflate WI-775's keying distinction: that governs a tuple TYPE's identity, whereas a destructuring binder list is not itself a tuple and claims only \"bind the i-th component\". DISTINCT from WI-784 (closure ARITY -- `enter_closure` is strictly unary and rejects an N-arg application outright); this one applies the closure with exactly ONE argument and fails later, inside pattern matching. Found while code-reviewing WI-783. ACCEPTANCE: a destructuring lambda over a name-keyed tuple evaluates, agreeing with the positional-tuple and named-operation spellings of the same call; binding is by SOURCE order, pinned by a case whose field names sort differently from their written order; arity strictness retained (an N-component tuple does not match an M-binder pattern for N != M); a labelled tuple sub-pattern binds the component carrying that label and fails to match on an unknown or already-covered label; full workspace green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-19T21:46:04Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-785", author: "user", content: "CORRECTION to this ticket's own description: the claimed 'SECOND SILENT GAP' -- that the dispatch arm dropped a tuple pattern's labelled sub-patterns, so '(x: a, y: b) bound nothing and still reported a match' -- is FALSE and was never a live bug. Verified four ways: the grammar has no labelled tuple-pattern element (`_tuple_pattern_elem` is `_pattern | typed_binder`, and grammar.js states `named_pattern_field` is 'used only inside constructor patterns'); the parser emits `pattern_tuple` with positional elements only (parse/convert.rs ~1894); the sole `Pattern::Tuple` producer hardcodes `named: Vec::new()` (kb/node_occurrence.rs ~2810); and the reflect encoding `tuple_pattern(elements: ...)` has no named channel. So `Pattern::Tuple.named` is ALWAYS EMPTY. Moreover '(x: a, y: b)' in tuple position parses as two WI-517 TYPED BINDERS (binders x,y at types a,b) which bound correctly before AND after -- measured. The delivered fix therefore no longer implements labelled sub-patterns; it refuses them with a debug_assert plus a documented no-match, since carrying untestable code for a shape no producer builds is worse than declining it. SECOND CORRECTION -- the first cut of this fix was UNSOUND and is now fixed: presenting components as `pos ++ named` is NOT source order, because `classify_ctor_arg` routes a component into `pos` iff its label starts with '_' (how the parser's synthetic _1/_2 return to pos), and a user label like '_b' takes the same branch. MEASURED on the first cut: `lambda (p, q) -> p - q` over `(a: 3, _b: 10)` returned 7 instead of -7; `(a:1,_b:2,c:3)` returned 213 instead of 123; and an operation declared '-> Int64' returned Str(\"ess\") on a clean load -- a type-soundness hole, converting a pre-fix LOUD raise into a silent wrong answer. The delivered fix now applies the widening ONLY where the split is unambiguous (one of pos/named empty) and refuses the mixed shape, whose interleaving is unrecoverable because `pos` keeps no labels. Two regression tests pin both (mixed_pos_and_named_tuple_refuses_rather_than_misbinding, mixed_tuple_cannot_smuggle_a_string_into_an_int_slot). Root cause filed as WI-786; when it lands, the mixed-shape refusal can be relaxed. Found by /code-review (4 of 10 angles independently built binaries and drove it); my original 5 tests were all all-positional or all-named and could not reach the shape.", at: "2026-07-20T05:42:59Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-785", author: "user", content: "WI-786 (delivered) removed this ticket's mixed-shape workaround. With classify_ctor_arg narrowed, `pos ++ named` is now guaranteed source order, so match_tuple_pattern no longer refuses a tuple split across both halves -- the refusal existed only because the split was unreliable. The two workaround tests moved to wi786_tuple_component_order_test.rs and now assert CORRECT binding instead of refusal. This file keeps the 5 tests about destructuring a name-keyed tuple.", at: "2026-07-20T06:29:39Z")
